# The resulting QDW mental model

I think this is the cleanest version yet:

```text
QDW DOES NOT STORE "GOOD IDEAS".

QDW stores reality.

Reality produces:
    tensions
    capabilities
    constraints
    resources
    needs

Those produce:
    opportunities

Opportunities produce:
    product hypotheses

Hypotheses produce:
    experiments/products

Products produce:
    outcomes

Outcomes alter:
    reality
```

And LifeGit is extremely important because it gives QDW a second kind of reality that the public Internet cannot provide:

```text
PUBLIC REALITY
what people publicly say/do

+

PRIVATE REALITY
what consenting people actually experience longitudinally

+

COMMISSIONED REALITY
what humans are paid to investigate/verify
```

That makes the HumanOracle concept from your attached architecture substantially more powerful: humans aren't an exception to QDW; **they are another SourceResult-producing sensor and fulfillment backend**, alongside APIs, models and GitHub. 

The branded products then become almost beautifully thin:

```text
lifegit.com
    = personal-space graph explorer

wrapped
    = temporal report projection

ideacemetery.com
    = IDEA semantic-object projection

myquestions
    = QUESTION projection

careergit
    = work-permission projection

painmap
    = aggregate TENSION projection

gitgoblin
    = technology/repository observation producer

alternativeapi
    = capability/resource projection + resolver

humanoracle
    = requirement fulfillment backend

cuntgoblin
    = allocator/controller over all of it
```

**That is the integration I'd freeze.** The next build should be the semantic core (`spaces + ontology + events/states + tensions + universal edges`) rather than another product-specific subsystem. Once that's present, LifeGit, Wrapped, Idea Cemetery and the reality marketplace become natural queries over QDW instead of parallel projects.
